WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.720
Many buffer overflows come from confusing capacity with current length.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.260
Capacity is the total space available.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.360
Length is how much meaningful data is currently present.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:16.780
A buffer with capacity for one hundred bytes may currently contain ten bytes, and a function that appends data needs to consider both values.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.800
Off-by-one errors often happen when code forgets space for a terminating null character.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.540
A C string needs storage for the content and storage for the terminator.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.920
If a buffer has room for sixteen bytes total, it cannot safely hold a sixteen-character C string plus a terminator.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.900
C strings require correct null termination, and not every byte sequence is a valid C string.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:46.600
Data from a network record, file block, device buffer, or binary protocol may contain embedded null bytes or may not include a terminator at all.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.860
Treating arbitrary bytes as a C string can make later operations read beyond intended data.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.320
Unchecked input can exceed assumptions.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:05.580
Values from files, sockets, environment variables, command-line arguments, devices, or other processes may be longer than expected, may use a different encoding, or may omit expected delimiters.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.560
Upstream validation is helpful only when the current code can rely on it and that reliance is documented.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.940
Truncation should be handled as a meaningful result, not silently ignored.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.600
Sometimes truncation is acceptable if the code records it and preserves a valid output.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.060
Sometimes it must be treated as an error.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.440
The dangerous habit is pretending a shortened value is the same as the complete value.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:36.900
Buffer safety requires clear contracts about input size, output capacity, resulting length, terminator behavior, and failure behavior.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:43.040
Those contracts should be visible at function boundaries, not hidden in comments far away from the copy or formatting operation.
